Yes—humans can make internally generated scenes feel surprisingly embodied.

Daydreaming, memory, rehearsal, dreams, VR, prayer, music, and intense imagination can all recruit some of the same perceptual, emotional, autonomic, and motor systems used during real experience. So a sufficiently vivid imagined scene can produce real tension, calm, grief, excitement, bodily sensations, and even changes in behavior afterward.

The important distinction is:

the experience is real; the simulated world is not necessarily externally real.

That gives imagination a lot of power without requiring us to confuse simulation with perception. You can use it as a sandbox: enter a state, explore it, rehearse decisions, extract patterns, then come back and compare the results against ordinary external evidence.

In your language, it is almost a voluntary internal theater with embodied stakes. The better the rendering, the more useful it can be—but the return path matters just as much as the entry path.